A||||8||loafer87gt||AAArgghh! Ticking dash noise||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||12-05-1999||09:29 PM||||Hi guys,<P>I know this is near to impossible to diagnose over the net, but I have a ticking, rubbing noise coming from my dash behind the sterring wheel. I don't know where it is coming from, I thought it was coming from the speaker, but that was not the case. It sounds like there is something tapping something else and it is driving me nuts. It does it most when I am at about 5 mph. When I stop it goes away. When I speed up it goes away. If anyone has ANY ideas, please reply. Thanks<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||12-05-1999||09:43 PM||||Never ever drive 5 mph.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||12-05-1999||11:11 PM||||Place your finger on the trip odometer button when you hear the noise and see if that's it.<BR>Mine took a spell a while back where it would stick when I reset it. pumped the button a few times and it would quit. may have been a bit of dirt inside.||||reg||1
Z||000003||loafer87gt||12-06-1999||12:14 AM||||Its not the odometer push button thingy. It a louder than that, sounds like something rubbing. Beets me, guess I'll have to take apart the dash one of these days.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||12-06-1999||12:14 AM||||Hey, I forgot about that, my 87 SE did that a couple times too and it does make a light clicking noise. But, why only at 5 mph? Road noise drowns out the sound maybe?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||12-06-1999||12:17 AM||||Maybe it's a bomb!!  Or maybe I've been watching too many James Bond movies?!?||||reg||4
Z||000006||Patrick||12-06-1999||12:29 AM||||<BR>I'll take a stab at it. <P>Any chance that it's the <B>heater/defogger fan</B>? There might be a way that the fan could be slowly turned by air simply flowing through the ducting when you start to move the car, and the blades are just ticking some piece of crap lodged in the fan housing. At greater speeds the fan may not contact the object.<P>Turn your fan on the slowest speed and see if you can still hear any other ticking sound. If you can, I'd say just follow batboy's advice. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000007||stsmithgt||12-06-1999||10:14 AM||||I'm not sure what moves the needle to the speedo on a Fiero, but I've had the mechanism behind the needle make noise like that before in other vehicles.  In some cases it was a dry speedo cable.  Does a Fiero even have a speedo cable back there?  A little motor maybe?||||reg||5
Z||000008||fiero56||12-06-1999||10:19 AM||||The speedo and odometer are both electronic.  They are electrically driven, no cables or anything mechanical about them. There is actually a fuse you can pull that will stop the speedo and odometer. I heard about this when a guy on another list asked why his odometer stopped working.  Turns out, the radio fuse also supplies the electricity to the speedometer and odometer. Kinda' makes you wonder if all Fiero's odometers are right on, or if their owners are pulling a fast one on the mileage.||||reg||14
